language ESSENCE' 1.0
$ circling_squares: an Essence' model from DCP Rosetta.
$
$ Circling the squares: place a different whole number in each of the ten squares of a circle, so that the sum of the squares of any two adjacent numbers equals the sum of the squares of the two numbers diametrically opposite them. The squares are, going round the circle, A, B, C, D, E, F, G, H, I, K. The numbers A = 16, B = 2, F = 8 and G = 14 stand as given, and fractions are not allowed.
$
$   https://dcp-bench.github.io/DCP-Rosetta/problems/circling_squares.html
$ Save this model as model.eprime and run it with Savile Row 1.11 (it needs Java); the solution is
$ written to `model.eprime.solution`:
$   savilerow model.eprime -run-solver

$ the ten numbers in the circle, going round in the order A, B, C, D, E, F, G, H, I, K; each a positive whole number of at most two figures (1 to 99): the puzzle says no number need contain more than two figures, so this bound is an assumption taken from that remark
$ A: the number in the first square
find A : int(1..99)
$ B: the number in the second square
find B : int(1..99)
$ C: the number in the third square
find C : int(1..99)
$ D: the number in the fourth square
find D : int(1..99)
$ E: the number in the fifth square
find E : int(1..99)
$ F: the number in the sixth square
find F : int(1..99)
$ G: the number in the seventh square
find G : int(1..99)
$ H: the number in the eighth square
find H : int(1..99)
$ I: the number in the ninth square
find I : int(1..99)
$ K: the number in the tenth square
find K : int(1..99)

such that
$ every number is different from every other number in the circle
allDiff([A, B, C, D, E, F, G, H, I, K]),
$ the numbers placed as examples stand as they are
A = 16,
B = 2,
F = 8,
G = 14,
$ A and B (adjacent) have the same total of squares as the diametrically opposite F and G
A * A + B * B = F * F + G * G,
$ B and C (adjacent) have the same total of squares as the diametrically opposite G and H
B * B + C * C = G * G + H * H,
$ C and D (adjacent) have the same total of squares as the diametrically opposite H and I
C * C + D * D = H * H + I * I,
$ D and E (adjacent) have the same total of squares as the diametrically opposite I and K
D * D + E * E = I * I + K * K,
$ E and F (adjacent) have the same total of squares as the diametrically opposite K and A
E * E + F * F = K * K + A * A,
$ F and G (adjacent) have the same total of squares as the diametrically opposite A and B
F * F + G * G = A * A + B * B,
$ G and H (adjacent) have the same total of squares as the diametrically opposite B and C
G * G + H * H = B * B + C * C,
$ H and I (adjacent) have the same total of squares as the diametrically opposite C and D
H * H + I * I = C * C + D * D,
$ I and K (adjacent) have the same total of squares as the diametrically opposite D and E
I * I + K * K = D * D + E * E,
$ K and A (adjacent) have the same total of squares as the diametrically opposite E and F
K * K + A * A = E * E + F * F
